    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "The short answer is **no: you should not make an issue of the panel’s composition, nor is it on its own a sign of bias or wrongdoing.** \n\nHere is a breakdown of why, along with how to evaluate the situation objectively and what steps to take next:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Panel Demographics Are Not Evidence of Bias\n* **Operational reality:** Selection panels in government ministries are usually composed based on availability, grade level, and relevant roles (e.g., hiring manager, peer division director, HR representative, external/independent assessor). It is very common for panels to end up single-gender purely by chance or organizational demographics.\n* **Legal standard:** An allegation of discrimination (whether informal or through a formal grievance) cannot be substantiated merely by noting the gender makeup of the panel. Without concrete evidence—such as departures from standard scoring criteria, procedural irregularities, or inappropriate remarks—pointing to the panel’s gender will appear speculative and could negatively affect your professional reputation within the civil service.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Qualifications vs. Fit at the Senior Level\n* **Paper qualifications vs. interview scoring:** On paper, both you and the successful candidate have strong qualifications. However, civil service hiring is strictly rubric- and merit-based. Final decisions rarely come down to whose résumé is longer; they are decided by who scored highest against the specific competencies during the interview stages.\n* **Strategic priorities:** A master’s in political science combined with demonstrable experience leading international projects may have directly aligned with specific upcoming priorities for that post. \n* **Interview performance:** Making the final three means you were considered fully capable of doing the job. At that stage, margins are typically very narrow, often decided by how well each candidate articulated vision, leadership, or handling of specific scenario-based questions.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay on Feedback\n* **Civil service timelines:** Two weeks is very normal for government HR processes. Panel members often need to review, consolidate, and sign off on feedback notes alongside their standard duties. \n* **Follow-up:** It is completely appropriate to send a brief, polite follow-up requesting an update on when feedback might be available.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a professional follow-up for feedback:**\n   > *\"Dear [HR / Hiring Contact], I am following up on my request for written feedback regarding the Senior Policy Adviser interviews. I would appreciate any insight the panel can provide to help inform my professional development and future applications. Thank you for your time.\"*\n\n2. **Scrutinize the substantive feedback once received:**\n   When you receive the written feedback, review your competency scores and notes objectively. Look for specific areas where the panel felt your answers fell short of the top mark (e.g., leadership examples, strategic alignment, or interview delivery).\n\n3. **Assess procedural fairness:**\n   If the written feedback indicates that non-merit criteria were used, or if the process breached formal civil service recruitment codes, you can explore formal review mechanisms at that point. Until then, treat this as a standard, highly competitive hiring outcome.\n",
